"Contributed Talks　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　Groups and Graphs, Designs and Dynamics (https://www.imm.uran.ru/rus/Postgraduate_education/Aspirantus/%D0%9C%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B3%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%20%D0%9D%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B9%20%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87/Program_book_G2D2.pdf)\nciteturn5search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.2 years ago; Crawled: 7.2 years ago; The deficiency δ of an extremal matrix *A* is the difference between the order and the weight of *A*. ... Taranenko, On the König–Hall–Egerváry theorem for multidimensional matrices and multipartite hypergraphs, ArXiv:1811.09981v1 [math.CO].\nJ. Combin.* 76 (2019) 104-116.\n\n**Anna Taranenko**　taa@math.nsc.ru  \nSobolev Institute of Mathematics, Novosibirsk, Russia\n\n[Friday August 16, 16:45]\n\nOn the König–Hall theorem for multidimensional matrices\n\nA *d*-dimensional matrix *A* of order *n* is a real multidimensional array of appropriate sizes. A hyperplane in *A* is a maximal (*d* − 1)-dimensional submatrix of the same order.  \nLet a polyplex *K* of weight *W* be a nonnegative multidimensional matrix in which the sum of entries over each hyperplane is not greater than 1 and the sum of all entries equals *W*. A polyplex of weight *n* and order *n* is said to be a polydiagonal. The simplest example of a polydiagonal is a diagonal matrix that is a (0, 1)-matrix with exactly one unity entry in each hyperplane.  \nWe will say that a multidimensional (0, 1)-matrix is extremal if it contains no polydiagonals and after changing any zero entry to a unity one a polydiagonal appears. The deficiency δ of an extremal matrix *A* is the difference between the order and the weight of *A*.  \nThe well-known König-Hall theorem can be stated as the following characterization result: all 2-dimensional extremal matrices of order *n* are exactly the (0, 1)-matrices whose zero entries form a (*k* × *l*)-submatrix with *k* + *l* = *n* + 1.  \nThe main aim of this talk is to study the characterization problem of multidimensional extremal matrices. For this purpose, we introduce optimal hyperplane covers that give us a convenient tool for operating with extremal matrices and put forward several conjectures on properties of extremal multidimensional matrices and their optimal hyperplane covers.\n\nConjecture 1. Every extremal matrix has the unique optimal hyperplane cover.  \nConjecture 2. The deficiency δ of any extremal matrix is equal to 1/*m* for some *m* ∈ ℕ.  \nConjecture 3. For any extremal matrix, changing a zero entry to a unity one produces a diagonal.\n\nWe confirm these conjectures with a series of constructions and examples. In particular, we obtain a full characterization of extremal matrices with small deficiencies and list all extremal matrices of small sizes.  \nThe work was funded by the Russian Science Foundation under grant 18-11-00136.\n\nReference\n\n[1] A.A. Taranenko, On the König–Hall–Egerváry theorem for multidimensional matrices and multipartite hypergraphs, ArXiv:1811.09981v1 [math.CO].\n\nAugust 12-25, 2019　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　60　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　Yichang, China--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbout S-packing coloring of subcubic graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012365X24000487)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; This parameter was introduced by Goddard et al. [8] under the name of broadcast chromatic number, and the authors showed that deciding whether ${\\chi }_{\\rho }(G)\\le 4$ is NP-hard. ... In section 3, we prove that every $(3,0)$-saturated subcubic graph is $(1,1,2,2)$-packing colorable.\n\nDate: May 2024\n\nArticle: 113917\n\nVolume: Volume 347, Issue 5\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# About S-packing coloring of subcubic graphs\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Maidoun Mortada ^{a}, Olivier Togni ^{b}\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2024.113917\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a non-decreasing sequence of integers $({a}_{1},{a}_{2},\\dots ,{a}_{k})$, an $({a}_{1},{a}_{2},\\dots ,{a}_{k})$-packing coloring of a graph G is a partition of $V(G)$ into k subsets ${V}_{1},{V}_{2},\\dots ,{V}_{k}$ such that the distance between any two distinct vertices $x,y\\in {V}_{i}$ is at least ${a}_{i}+1$, $1\\le i\\le k$. This paper studies the packing coloring of subcubic graphs. Gastineau and Togni (2016) [7] asked whether every subcubic graph except the Petersen graph is $(1,1,2,3)$-packing colorable. A subcubic graph G is said to be i-saturated, $0\\le i\\le 2$, if every vertex of degree three in G has at most i neighbors of degree three and it is said to be 3-saturated if it is not 2-saturated. A graph G is said to be subcubic if $\\mathrm{\\Delta }(G)\\le 3$.\n\nLet G be a subcubic graph. A vertex x in G is said to be a 2-vertex if $d(x)=2$ and 3-vertex if $d(x)=3$. A 3-vertex in G is said to be a heavy vertex if all its neighbors are 3-vertices. We classify the subcubic graphs into four classes:\n\n  * •\n\nG is said to be 0-saturated if any two 3-vertices in G are not adjacent. This type is also called 3-irregular subcubic graph in [7], [14].\n\n  * •\n\nG is said to be 1-saturated if every 3-vertex in G is adjacent to at most one 3-vertex.\n\n  * •\n\nG is said to be 2-saturated if every 3-vertex in G is adjacent to at most two 3-vertices.\n\n  * •\n\nG is said to be 3-saturated if G is not 2-saturated.\n\nFor the class of 3-saturated subcubic graphs, we consider, for $0\\le i\\le 3$, the subclass of $(3,i)$-saturated subcubics graphs, which consists of the 3-saturated subcubic graphs such that every heavy vertex is adjacent to at most i heavy vertices.\n\nIf $({a}_{1},{a}_{2},\\dots ,{a}_{k})$ is a non-decreasing sequence of positive integers, then an $({a}_{1},{a}_{2},\\dots ,{a}_{k})$-packing coloring of a graph G is a partition of $V(G)$ into k subsets ${V}_{1},{V}_{2},\\dots ,{V}_{k}$ such that for each two distinct vertices x and y in {V}_{i}${V}_{i}$, $dist(x,y)>{a}_{i}$ for $1\\le i\\le k$. The smallest k such that G has a $(1,2,\\dots ,k)$-packing coloring is called the packing chromatic number of G and is denoted by ${\\chi }_{\\rho }(G)$. This parameter was introduced by Goddard et al. [8] under the name of broadcast chromatic number, and the authors showed that deciding whether ${\\chi }_{\\rho }(G)\\le 4$ is NP-hard. For a graph G, let $S(G)$ denote the graph obtained from G by subdividing every edge. Finding a bound of ${\\chi }_{\\rho }(G)$ and ${\\chi }_{\\rho }(S(G))$ for a subcubic graph G was studied in many papers [2], [4], [5], [6], [7], [10], [12]. The question of the boundedness of {\\chi }_{\\rho }${\\chi }_{\\rho }$ for this class was settled in the negative by Balogh, Kostochka and Liu [1], and explicit constructions given by Brešar and Ferme [3]. Besides, Balogh, Kostochka and Liu [2] showed ${\\chi }_{\\rho }(S(G))$ is bounded by 8 in the class of subcubic graphs. Gastineau and Togni [7] asked whether ${\\chi }_{\\rho }(S(G))\\le 5$. Then Brešar, Klavžar, Rall, and Wash [6] conjectured that ${\\chi }_{\\rho }(S(G))\\le 5$ for every subcubic graph G. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Multidimensional threshold matrices and extremal matrices of order $2 (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/364689703_Multidimensional_threshold_matrices_and_extremal_matrices_of_order_2)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Anna Taranenko ... [9, Conjecture 3.7] The deﬁciency δ of any extremal matrix is equal to 1 ... By [9, Theorem 6.6], d-dimensional matrices of diversity 1 have deﬁciency δ=1\n\n# Multidimensional threshold matrices and extremal matrices of order $2\n\n  * October 2022\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2210.12405\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nAnna Taranenko\n\n  * Sobolev Institute of Mathematics\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe paper is devoted to multidimensional (0,1)-matrices extremal with respect to containing a polydiagonal (a fractional generalization of a diagonal). Every extremal matrix is a threshold matrix, i.e., an entry belongs to its support whenever a weighted sum of incident hyperplanes exceeds a given threshold. Firstly, we prove that nonequivalent threshold matrices have different distributions of ones in hyperplanes. Next, we establish that extremal matrices of order 2 are exactly selfdual threshold Boolean functions. Using this fact, we find the asymptotics of the number of extremal matrices of order 2 and provide counterexamples to several conjectures on extremal matrices. Finally, we describe extremal matrices of order 2 with a small diversity of hyperplanes.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nAvailable via license: CC BY 4.0\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\narXiv:2210.12405v1 [math.CO] 22 Oct 2022\n\nMultidimensional threshold matrices and extremal\n\nmatrices of order 2\n\nA. A. Taranenko∗\n\nAbstract\n\nThe paper is devoted to multidimensional (0,1)-matrices extremal with respect to con-\n\ntaining a polydiagonal (a fractional generalization of a diagonal). Every extremal matrix is a\n\nthreshold matrix, i.e., an entry belongs to its support whenever a weighted sum of incident\n\nhyperplanes exceeds a given threshold.\n\nFirstly, we prove that nonequivalent threshold matrices have diﬀerent distributions of\n\nones in hyperplanes. Next, we establish that extremal matrices of order 2 are exactly self-\n\ndual threshold Boolean functions. Using this fact, we ﬁnd the asymptotics of the number\n\nof extremal matrices of order 2 and provide counterexamples to several conjectures on ex-\n\ntremal matrices. Finally, we describe extremal matrices of order 2 with a small diversity of\n\nhyperplanes.\n\nKeywords: multidimensional matrix, extremal matrix, threshold matrix, selfdual Boolean\n\nfunction.\n\nIntroduction\n\nA d-dimensional (0,1)-matrix A of order n is an array (a α)α∈I d\n\na α= 1 ⇔X\n\ni,j\n\nℓ i,j v α\n\ni,j ≥1.\n\nTheorem 2. [9, Theorem 4.1] Every extremal matrix A is equivalent to a threshold matrix.\n\nMoreover, if Λ is an optimal hyperplane cover of an extremal matrix A, then A=A(Λ).\n\nMost results of paper [9] were motivated by the following conjectures.\n\nConjecture 3. [9, Conjecture 3.4] Every extremal matrix has a unique optimal cover.\n\nConjecture 4. [9, Conjecture 3.9] If Λ is an optimal hyperplane cover of an extremal matrix\n\nof deﬁciency δ, then all entries λ i,j are integer multiples of δ.\n\nConjecture 5. [9, Conjecture 3.7] The deﬁciency δ of any extremal matrix is equal to 1\n\n/m for\n\nsome m∈N.\n\nIn this paper we provide counterexamples to Conjectures 3 and 4 in the class of extremal\n\nmatrices of order 2. Meanwhile, in Section 3 we show that all of these conjectures are true for\n\nextremal matrices of small diversity of hyperplanes.\n\n2 Threshold functions and bounds on the number of ex-\n\ntremal matrices\n\nSuppose that A is a d-dimensional (0,1)-matrix of order n. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n262  BECOMING A DEVELOPMENT CATEGORY (https://www.math.utoronto.ca/mpugh/Shrestha_1995_Becoming_a_development_category.pdf)\nciteturn5search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.1 years ago; They wore two disparate faces: one looking meek and saying hajur, hajur (yes sir, yes sir) to those above them and another stern and rude, treating those below them as worthless subhumans.\nSeveral wealthy families in Pokhara sent their children to these two schools, both located in Kathmandu Valley. When these children, some of whom lived in my neighbourhood, came back home during breaks, we could hear them speak fluent English. They would have little contact with us, and sometimes treat us like pakhes (uncivilized). Educationally, we felt very deficient in front of those elite children. The new education was preparing a new generation who not only controlled the rapidly expanding bureaucracy, but also dominated the development enterprise, thereby reaping the lopsided benefits of bikas. Education and bikas both not only displayed a distinct class character, but also accentuated the prevailing class biases of colonial society. Most educated people shunned hard work and looked for work in the civil service sector where they could boss their juniors around. They wore two disparate faces: one looking meek and saying hajur, hajur (yes sir, yes sir) to those above them and another stern and rude, treating those below them as worthless subhumans.\n\nBy the mid-1950s, the idea of bikas had been firmly transplanted in the Nepalese psyche. Whether bikas was actually occurring did not matter. It had--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn $S$-packing Coloring of Bounded Degree Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18793)\nciteturn5academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; We prove that any graph of maximum degree $k\\ge 3$ is $(1^{k-1}, 3)$-packing colorable if it is 0-saturated, $(1^{k-1}, 2)$-packing colorable if it is $t$-saturated, $1\\leq t\\leq k-2$; and $(1^{k-1},2^{k-1})$-packing colorable if it is $(k-1)$-saturated.\nTitle: On $S$-packing Coloring of Bounded Degree Graphs\nAuthors: Maidoun Mortada, Olivier Togni\nDate: Mon Mar 24 15:39:35 2025\n\nGiven a sequence $S=(s_1,s_2,\\ldots,s_p)$, $p\\geq 2$, of non-decreasing integers, an $S$-packing coloring of a graph $G$ is a partition of its vertex set into $p$ disjoint sets $V_1,\\ldots, V_p$ such that any two distinct vertices of $V_i$ are at a distance greater than $s_i$, $1\\le i\\le p$. In this paper, we study the $S$-packing coloring problem on graphs of bounded maximum degree and for sequences mainly containing 1's and 2's ($i^r$ in a sequence means $i$ is repeated $r$ times). Generalizing existing results for subcubic graphs, we prove a series of results on graphs of maximum degree $k$: We show that graphs of maximum degree $k$ are $(1^{k-1},2^k)$-packing colorable. Moreover, we refine this result for restricted subclasses: A graph of maximum degree $k$ is said to be $t$-saturated, $0\\le t\\le k$, if every vertex of degree $k$ is adjacent to at most $t$ vertices of degree $k$. We prove that any graph of maximum degree $k\\ge 3$ is $(1^{k-1}, 3)$-packing colorable if it is 0-saturated, $(1^{k-1}, 2)$-packing colorable if it is $t$-saturated, $1\\leq t\\leq k-2$; and $(1^{k-1},2^{k-1})$-packing colorable if it is $(k-1)$-saturated. We also propose some conjectures and questions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nÈçâåñòèÿ âóçîâ. Ìàòåìàòèêà (https://www.mathnet.ru/links/393cfb3f9bacec743db7c96e8db4949b/ivm9490.pdf)\nciteturn5search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.3 years ago; Conjectures involving a generalization of the sums of powers of integers, Exp. ... Research problems: a conjectured representation of Genocchi numbers, Amer. ... Proof of a conjecture on Genocchi numbers, Disc.\nj ñ÷èòàåì íóëåâîé.\nËèòåðàòóðà\n[1] Ãðýõåì Ç., Êíóò Ä., Ïàòàøíèê Î. Êîíêðåòíàÿ ìàòåìàòèêà. Îñíîâàíèå èíôîðìàòèêè (Ìèð, Ì., 1998).\n[2] Knuth D. E. Johann Faulhaber and the sums of powers, Math. Comp. 61 (203), 277\u0015294 (1993).\n[3] Jacobi C. G. De usu legitimoformulae summatoriae Maclaurinaianae, J. Reine Angew. Math. 12, 263\u0015272\n(1834).\n[4] Svinin A. K. Conjectures involving a generalization of the sums of powers of integers, Exp. Math. doi:\n10.1080/10586458.2017.1306815.\n[5] Strazdins I. Solution to problem B-871, Fibonacci Quart. 38.1, 86\u001587 (2000).\n[6] Tuenter H. J. H. Walking into an absolute sum, Fibonac